window.COURSE_TREE=[{"id":"foundations","name":"Foundations","cats":[{"name":"The big picture","s":[[1,"What is a large language model? The big picture","big-picture",1,"An LLM is a function that gives a probability for the next token · The life of a model: pretrain → post-train → serve → use · Why one model can do many tasks: classification, summarization, chat · Where images and video join the same picture"]]},{"name":"Math, optimization & PyTorch","s":[[2,"Math you need, part 1: vectors, dot products, matrices and softmax","math-vectors-matrices-softmax",1,"Vectors as lists of features; the dot product as a similarity score · Matrix multiply = many dot products at once; the shape rule (B, T, d) · Softmax turns scores into probabilities; temperature makes it sharp or flat · Numerical stability: subtract the max before exp"],[3,"Math you need, part 2: probability, cross-entropy, KL divergence and perplexity","math-probability-cross-entropy",1,"Maximum likelihood = minimize the negative log-likelihood · Cross-entropy between data and model; KL as the extra cost of a wrong model · Perplexity = exp(average loss) · Nats vs bits; a loss of 2.0 nats means perplexity ≈ 7.4"],[4,"Optimization: backpropagation, SGD, Adam and AdamW, learning-rate schedules","optimization-backprop-adamw",1,"Backprop = the chain rule run backwards on a graph · SGD vs momentum vs Adam; why AdamW separates weight decay · Warmup + cosine decay, and warmup-stable-decay (WSD) · How batch size, learning rate and gradient noise interact"],[5,"Lab: PyTorch mental model and a tiny bigram language model","lab-pytorch-bigram",1,"Tensors, dtypes, devices, the autograd graph, nn.Module · A clean, deterministic training loop: seeds, eval mode, no_grad · Train a bigram and a small MLP language model on short text · Read the loss curve, sample text, compute perplexity"]]},{"name":"Language modeling before transformers","s":[[6,"Language modeling: next-token prediction, from n-grams to neural models","next-token-prediction",1,"Chain rule: the probability of a text is a product of next-token probabilities · Autoregressive generation: predict, append, repeat · n-gram models as the simple baseline, and why they fail (sparsity, short memory) · Teacher forcing in training vs free running at inference"],[7,"Before transformers: RNN, LSTM, seq2seq and the first attention","before-transformers-rnn-lstm-seq2seq-and-the",1,"RNN hidden state as a running summary; vanishing gradients · LSTM gates as memory control · Seq2seq encoder–decoder and its one-vector bottleneck · Bahdanau attention: the decoder looks back at all encoder states. Transformers keep this idea."]]},{"name":"Tokenization","s":[[8,"What is a token? Subword tokenization (BPE, WordPiece, Unigram)","what-is-a-token-subword-tokenization-bpe",1,"Characters vs words vs subwords: vocabulary size vs sequence length · Byte-level BPE step by step: count pairs, merge the most common, repeat · WordPiece and Unigram (SentencePiece): how they differ · Effects of vocabulary size: embedding size, output layer cost, tokens per word"],[9,"Special tokens, chat templates and tokenizer pitfalls","special-tokens-chat-templates-and-tokenizer-pitfalls",1,"BOS, EOS, PAD, UNK; BERT’s CLS, SEP, MASK: what each one is for · Chat templates: role tokens (system, user, assistant), tool-call tokens, thinking tokens · Placeholder tokens for images, audio and video in multimodal models · Pitfalls: numbers, spaces, glitch tokens, and multilingual cost (Farsi or Arabic often need more tokens per word than English)"],[10,"Lab: Build a byte-level BPE tokenizer from scratch","lab-build-a-byte-level-bpe-tokenizer",1,"Train BPE on a small corpus; check that encode then decode gives the same text · Compare with tiktoken and a Hugging Face tokenizer · Measure tokens per word in English and in another language you speak · Read a real chat template and find its special tokens"]]},{"name":"Embeddings & position","s":[[11,"Embeddings: turning token IDs into vectors","embeddings-turning-token-ids-into-vectors",1,"Embedding layer = one-hot × matrix = table lookup · word2vec intuition: words in similar contexts get similar vectors · Cosine similarity and the geometry of meaning, and its limits · Embedding size and parameter count: |V| × d"],[12,"Contextual embeddings, hidden states and the output layer","contextual-embeddings-hidden-states-and-the-output",1,"Static vs contextual: “bank” (river) and “bank” (money) get different hidden states · Hidden states layer by layer; what “the embedding of a sentence” can mean · Unembedding: hidden state → logits over the vocabulary; weight tying · Sentence embeddings from a model: CLS, mean pooling, last token"],[13,"Position, part 1: why order needs help; absolute and relative positions","position-part-1-why-order-needs-help",1,"Self-attention ignores order: without positions, “dog bites man” = “man bites dog” · Sinusoidal and learned absolute positions · Relative positions: T5 bias buckets and ALiBi linear bias · What happens past the training length"],[14,"Position, part 2: RoPE in depth, and a first look at 2D and 3D RoPE","position-part-2-rope-in-depth-and",1,"Rotate each pair of dimensions by an angle that grows with position · Why the score depends only on the distance m − n · Frequencies θ_{i}, the base (10000 or larger), long and short wavelengths · Preview: 2D RoPE for image patches and M-RoPE for video (time, height, width)"],[15,"Lab: Embeddings and RoPE by hand","lab-embeddings-and-rope-by-hand",1,"Plot trained embeddings with PCA; find nearest neighbors · Implement RoPE; test with numbers that scores depend only on relative position · Plot RoPE frequencies; see which dimensions handle long and short distances"]]}]},{"id":"transformers","name":"Attention and transformers","cats":[{"name":"Attention","s":[[16,"Attention intuition: query, key, value","attention-intuition-query-key-value",1,"Attention as a soft dictionary lookup · Query = what I look for; key = the label I show; value = what I give if chosen · Why three different projections W_{Q}, W_{K}, W_{V} · Attention weights as a probability distribution over positions"],[17,"Scaled dot-product attention: the math and the shapes","scaled-dot-product-attention-the-math-and",1,"Q = XW_{Q}, K = XW_{K}, V = XW_{V}, each of shape (T, d_{k}) · Scores QKᵀ, scale by √d_{k}, softmax over keys, weighted sum of V · Why √d_{k}: the variance of a dot product grows with d_{k} and saturates softmax · Cost: O(T²·d) compute and O(T²) memory for the score matrix"],[18,"Masks: causal, padding, bidirectional, prefix and packing masks","masks-causal-padding-bidirectional-prefix-and-packing",1,"Causal mask: a token sees only itself and the past · Padding mask for batches with different lengths · Bidirectional (BERT) vs causal (GPT) vs prefix-LM (partly bidirectional) · Document masks when many samples are packed into one sequence"],[19,"Multi-head attention","multi-head-attention",1,"Split d_{model} into h heads of size d_{k} = d_{model}/h · Each head can learn a different relation: syntax, coreference, position · Concatenate the heads, then the output projection W_{O} · Parameters: 4·d_{model}² per attention layer (without biases)"],[20,"Self-attention vs cross-attention","self-attention-vs-cross-attention",1,"Self-attention: Q, K and V come from the same sequence · Cross-attention: Q from one sequence (text), K and V from another (encoder output, image, audio) · Encoder–decoder attention in translation and speech recognition · Why cross-attention is the base of some VLM designs (Flamingo, Llama 3.2 Vision)"]]},{"name":"Inside the transformer block","s":[[21,"The feed-forward network (MLP) inside each block","the-feed-forward-network-mlp-inside-each",1,"Expand (d → 4d), non-linearity, project back (4d → d) · GELU, and gated units (GLU, SwiGLU) in modern LLMs · The FFN as a key–value memory where many facts seem to be stored · About 2/3 of the parameters of a dense block are in the FFN"],[22,"The residual stream and normalization","the-residual-stream-and-normalization",1,"Residual connections: each layer adds a small edit to a shared stream · LayerNorm vs RMSNorm: what each removes, and why RMSNorm is cheaper · Post-LN (original) vs Pre-LN (modern): stability and warmup · QK-norm and other fixes for growing attention logits"],[23,"The full transformer and its three families","the-full-transformer-and-its-three-families",1,"Encoder-only (BERT): understanding tasks, embeddings, classification · Decoder-only (GPT, Llama, Qwen): generation and chat; almost all modern LLMs · Encoder–decoder (T5, Whisper): input-to-output tasks like translation and speech · How to choose for a task, and why decoder-only won at scale"],[24,"Encoder–decoder in depth: T5 span corruption, sentinel tokens, BART denoising","encoder-decoder-in-depth-t5-span-corruption",1,"T5: every task is text-to-text; span corruption with sentinel tokens <X>, <Y> · How the decoder uses cross-attention to the encoder at every step · BART: corrupt the text (mask, delete, shuffle), then rebuild it · When encoder–decoders still win: translation, summarization, speech"],[25,"Counting: parameters, FLOPs and memory of a transformer","counting-parameters-flops-and-memory-of-a",1,"Parameters per layer ≈ 12·d² (attention 4d² + FFN 8d²), plus embeddings · Forward ≈ 2N FLOPs per token; training ≈ 6N FLOPs per token, so 6ND in total · The extra attention term grows with context length · Worked example: estimate an 8B model, then check it against its config file"]]},{"name":"Attention variants, long context & MoE","s":[[26,"Attention variants 1: MHA, MQA, GQA, MLA and the KV cache","attention-variants-1-mha-mqa-gqa-mla",1,"MQA: all heads share one K and one V · GQA: groups of heads share K and V; the standard choice today · MLA (DeepSeek): compress K and V into a small latent vector · Effect on KV-cache memory and on quality"],[27,"Attention variants 2: a first look at sliding windows, linear attention and SSMs","attention-variants-2-a-first-look-at",1,"Sliding-window attention (Mistral) and local + global patterns · Linear attention: the kernel trick, O(T) cost, and what it loses · State space models (Mamba) as a recurrent alternative · Attention sinks. All of these get a full deep dive in Part C."],[28,"What “context” means, and how to make it longer","what-context-means-and-how-to-make",1,"Context window vs training length vs effective (really usable) context · Extending RoPE: position interpolation, NTK-aware scaling, YaRN · Lost in the middle; needle-in-a-haystack and RULER tests · Context vs memory vs retrieval: three ways to give a model more information"],[29,"Mixture of Experts (MoE)","mixture-of-experts-moe",1,"A router picks the top-k experts for each token; only those FFNs run · Total vs active parameters: cheap per token, heavy in memory · Load-balancing loss, capacity factor, dropped tokens, shared experts · A first look at expert parallelism"]]},{"name":"Lab","s":[[30,"Lab: Write a GPT from scratch and train it","lab-write-a-gpt-from-scratch-and",1,"Build embeddings, causal multi-head attention, FFN, RMSNorm, residuals, LM head · Count the parameters and compare with the formula from session 24 · Train on a small corpus; plot train and validation loss; sample with temperature · Break it on purpose: remove positions, remove √d, use Post-LN. Explain what fails."]]}]},{"id":"pretraining","name":"Pretraining and scale","cats":[{"name":"Objectives, data & scaling","s":[[31,"Pretraining objectives","pretraining-objectives",1,"Causal LM (next token) and its loss · Masked LM (BERT): predict hidden tokens using both sides · Span corruption (T5) and prefix-LM · Why next-token prediction learns so much: the compression view"],[32,"One training step, end to end: forward, loss, backward, update","one-training-step-end-to-end-forward",1,"Follow one batch: tokens → embeddings → blocks → logits → loss · Backward: where every gradient comes from, layer by layer · Optimizer update, gradient clipping, learning-rate schedule · What lives in GPU memory at each moment of the step"],[33,"Encoders for understanding: BERT, RoBERTa, ALBERT and the CLS token","encoders-for-understanding-bert-roberta-albert-and",1,"BERT input: [CLS] sentence A [SEP] sentence B [SEP]; MLM + next-sentence prediction · RoBERTa: more data, dynamic masking, no NSP, bigger batches · ALBERT: cross-layer parameter sharing and factorized embeddings · The CLS token vs mean pooling; a classification head is one linear layer + softmax"],[34,"Pretraining data: from web crawl to training mixture","pretraining-data-from-web-crawl-to-training",1,"Sources: web, books, code, papers, math, many languages · Cleaning and filtering: language ID, quality classifiers, rules · Deduplication: exact hashes and MinHash for near-duplicates · Data mixture and curriculum; synthetic data; contamination risk"],[35,"Scaling laws","scaling-laws",1,"Loss falls as a power law in parameters, data and compute · Kaplan vs Chinchilla: why the best tokens-per-parameter changed (about 20) · Compute-optimal vs inference-optimal: train small models on much more data · Using scaling laws to plan a training run"],[36,"Training recipe and stability","training-recipe-and-stability",1,"Initialization, and why scale matters · Warmup, cosine and warmup-stable-decay schedules · Loss spikes: causes and fixes (gradient clipping, z-loss, QK-norm, lower LR) · μP: tune on a small model, transfer the settings to a big one"]]},{"name":"Precision & memory","s":[[37,"Numbers on GPUs: FP32, TF32, FP16, BF16, FP8 and mixed precision","numbers-on-gpus-fp32-tf32-fp16-bf16",1,"Sign, exponent and mantissa bits: range vs precision · Why FP16 needs loss scaling and BF16 usually does not · Mixed precision: low-precision math, FP32 master weights, FP32 accumulation · FP8 training basics (E4M3 and E5M2) and scaling factors"],[38,"Memory math for training","memory-math-for-training",1,"Weights, gradients, optimizer states: 16 bytes per parameter for mixed-precision Adam · Activation memory: how it grows with batch, sequence length and layers · Why full fine-tuning of a 7B model does not fit on one 24 GB GPU · Where memory really goes: fragmentation, buffers, temporary tensors"],[39,"Gradient accumulation and gradient checkpointing","gradient-accumulation-and-gradient-checkpointing",1,"Gradient accumulation: many small batches, one optimizer step; the effective batch size · Scale the loss correctly across steps (and the token-count trap) · Gradient (activation) checkpointing: drop activations, recompute them in backward · Selective recomputation; the cost is about one extra forward pass"],[40,"Evaluating base models","evaluating-base-models",1,"Perplexity is only comparable with the same tokenizer and data · Benchmarks (MMLU, HellaSwag, GSM8K) and how scoring works: log-likelihood vs generation · Contamination: when test data leaks into training · Using an evaluation harness correctly"],[41,"Lab: Measure memory, accumulation and checkpointing","lab-measure-memory-accumulation-and-checkpointing",1,"Profile peak memory of a ~1B model at different batch sizes · Turn on BF16, checkpointing and accumulation; record speed and memory · Check that accumulation gives the same gradients as one big batch · Write a memory calculator and compare it with your measurements"]]},{"name":"Distributed training & GPUs","s":[[42,"GPU architecture for ML","gpu-architecture-for-ml",1,"SMs, warps and tensor cores · Memory hierarchy: registers, shared memory (SRAM), L2, HBM or GDDR · The roofline: compute-bound vs memory-bound; arithmetic intensity · MFU: how much of the GPU you really use"],[43,"Collective communication: all-reduce, reduce-scatter, all-gather","collective-communication-all-reduce-reduce-scatter-all",1,"What each collective does, with pictures · Ring all-reduce = reduce-scatter + all-gather; its cost · NCCL; NVLink vs PCIe vs InfiniBand and Ethernet · Overlapping communication with compute"],[44,"Data parallelism (DDP)","data-parallelism-ddp",1,"Copy the model to every GPU, split the batch, average the gradients · Gradient bucketing and overlap with the backward pass · The limit: every GPU holds the full model and optimizer state · Seeds and determinism across ranks"],[45,"ZeRO stages 1, 2 and 3","zero-stages-1-2-and-3",1,"Stage 1 shards optimizer states; stage 2 adds gradients; stage 3 adds parameters · Memory per GPU for each stage · Communication cost vs memory saved · Offloading to CPU or NVMe"],[46,"FSDP (version 1)","fsdp-version-1",1,"FlatParameter: flatten and shard each wrapped unit · Forward: all-gather → compute → free. Backward: all-gather → gradients → reduce-scatter · Wrapping policy, prefetching, sharding strategies (FULL_SHARD, SHARD_GRAD_OP, HYBRID_SHARD) · Mixed precision and CPU offload options"],[47,"FSDP2: per-parameter sharding with DTensor","fsdp2-per-parameter-sharding-with-dtensor",1,"The fully_shard API: each parameter is a DTensor sharded on dim 0 · Why it is simpler than FlatParameter: clean state dicts, easy freezing and LoRA · MixedPrecisionPolicy, reshard_after_forward, prefetching; works with torch.compile and tensor parallel · Moving from FSDP1 to FSDP2; common bugs"],[48,"Tensor parallelism and sequence parallelism","tensor-parallelism-and-sequence-parallelism",1,"Megatron-style column-parallel and row-parallel linear layers · Attention heads split across GPUs; two all-reduces per layer in forward (after attention, after MLP) · Sequence parallelism for the LayerNorm and dropout regions · Why TP needs fast NVLink and usually stays inside one node"],[49,"Pipeline, context and expert parallelism; 3D and 4D parallel","pipeline-context-and-expert-parallelism-3d-and",1,"Pipeline parallel: split layers into stages; micro-batches; GPipe vs 1F1B; bubbles · Context parallel: split the sequence (ring attention, Ulysses) · Expert parallel for MoE (all-to-all) · How big runs combine DP × TP × PP × CP"],[50,"Making training fast: compile, CUDA graphs, fused kernels, profiling","making-training-fast-compile-cuda-graphs-fused",1,"torch.compile: graph capture, fusion, recompiles and graph breaks · CUDA graphs remove kernel-launch overhead · Fused kernels: attention, RMSNorm, cross-entropy · Reading a PyTorch profiler or Nsight Systems trace: idle gaps and memory copies"],[51,"Distributed checkpoints, fault tolerance and MFU","distributed-checkpoints-fault-tolerance-and-mfu",1,"Sharded (distributed) checkpoints: save and load with many GPUs · Failures at scale: restart, elastic training, silent data corruption · Model FLOPs utilization (MFU): how to compute and read it · Overlapping communication with compute"],[52,"Lab: DDP vs FSDP2 and reading a profile","lab-ddp-vs-fsdp2-and-reading-a",1,"Train the same model with DDP and with FSDP2 (2 or more GPUs; or a small simulation) · Compare memory per GPU, step time and communication · Capture a profiler trace and find the top 3 time sinks · Combine activation checkpointing with FSDP2"]]}]},{"id":"posttraining","name":"Post-training and RL","cats":[{"name":"Fine-tuning & PEFT","s":[[53,"From base model to assistant","from-base-model-to-assistant",1,"What a base model does vs what an assistant does · The stages: SFT → preference tuning → RL · Instruction data: human-written, synthetic (self-instruct), distilled · Chat templates, and why a wrong template breaks a model"],[54,"SFT in detail","sft-in-detail",1,"Loss only on the assistant tokens (loss masking) · Packing many examples into one sequence with document masks · Epochs, learning rate, sequence length; signs of overfitting · Data quality vs quantity; diversity"],[55,"Fine-tuning for classic NLP tasks","fine-tuning-for-classic-nlp-tasks",1,"Sequence classification: sentiment (positive or negative documents), topic, intent · Token classification: named entities, tagging · Sequence-to-sequence: summarization, translation · Encoder or decoder for each task; class imbalance; metrics (F1, ROUGE)"],[56,"The PEFT family: adapters, BitFit, prefix tuning, prompt tuning, P-Tuning v2","the-peft-family-adapters-bitfit-prefix-tuning",1,"Adapters: small bottleneck layers inside each block (Houlsby) · BitFit: train only the biases · Prompt tuning and prefix tuning: learn virtual tokens or per-layer key/value prefixes; P-Tuning and P-Tuning v2 · How to choose: prompting vs linear probe vs PEFT vs full fine-tuning (data size, latency, number of tasks)"],[57,"LoRA and other PEFT methods","lora-and-other-peft-methods",1,"LoRA: freeze W and learn a low-rank update BA · Rank r, the α/r scale, which layers to adapt (attention, MLP, all linear layers) · Merging adapters for zero extra inference cost; serving many adapters · DoRA, prefix tuning, prompt tuning: when they help"],[58,"QLoRA and training with quantized weights","qlora-and-training-with-quantized-weights",1,"NF4 4-bit base weights with BF16 compute · Double quantization of the scale factors · Paged optimizers to survive memory spikes · Quality vs memory, and when QLoRA is not enough"],[59,"Knowledge distillation","knowledge-distillation",1,"Soft targets and temperature · Logit distillation (KL) vs sequence-level distillation (train on teacher outputs) · Distilling reasoning traces into small models · When the student cannot copy the teacher (the capacity gap)"],[60,"Training embedding and retrieval models","training-embedding-and-retrieval-models",1,"Contrastive learning: pull positives together, push negatives apart · InfoNCE and temperature; in-batch negatives; hard negatives · Bi-encoder training (Sentence-BERT, E5) and how to evaluate it · The same idea as CLIP: our bridge to vision"],[61,"Forgetting, data mixing and model merging","forgetting-data-mixing-and-model-merging",1,"Catastrophic forgetting: why fine-tuning can break general skills · Mix in general data, use a lower LR, or use LoRA as a regularizer · Model merging: weight averaging, model soups, task vectors · How to measure regressions"],[62,"Lab: LoRA vs QLoRA vs full fine-tuning","lab-lora-vs-qlora-vs-full-fine",1,"Fine-tune a small LM for sentiment (positive vs negative documents) · Fine-tune it for summarization; evaluate with ROUGE and an LLM judge · Compare memory, speed and quality for full FT, LoRA and QLoRA · Write down what you would ship, and why"]]},{"name":"RL foundations","s":[[63,"Reinforcement learning words, for LLMs","reinforcement-learning-words-for-llms",1,"Agent, environment, state, action, reward, return, episode · Text generation as RL: state = prompt + text so far, action = next token, policy = the LLM · Sparse rewards that come only at the end of an answer · Why RL and not only SFT: learn from a score, not from a copy"],[64,"Policy gradient and REINFORCE","policy-gradient-and-reinforce",1,"The log-derivative trick: ∇E[R] = E[R ∇log π] · REINFORCE for a whole generated answer · High variance, and baselines that add no bias · Advantage = how much better than expected"],[65,"Value functions, advantage and GAE","value-functions-advantage-and-gae",1,"State value V(s) and action value Q(s, a) · The TD error; the bias–variance trade-off · Generalized Advantage Estimation with γ and λ · Actor–critic: a policy plus a learned critic"],[66,"PPO","ppo",1,"The importance ratio between the new and old policy · The clipped objective keeps each update small · KL penalty to a frozen reference model; value loss; entropy bonus · Four models in memory: policy, reference, reward model, critic"]]},{"name":"Preference optimization","s":[[67,"RLHF: preference data and reward models","rlhf-preference-data-and-reward-models",1,"Collect pairs: which answer is better? · The Bradley–Terry reward model · PPO against the reward model, with a KL leash · Reward hacking and over-optimization (Goodhart’s law)"],[68,"DPO: alignment without a separate reward model","dpo-alignment-without-a-separate-reward-model",1,"Start from the KL-regularized RL objective · Its closed-form best policy turns the reward into a log-ratio · Put that into Bradley–Terry and you get the DPO loss · β, the reference model, and what DPO can and cannot do (offline data)"],[69,"The DPO family: IPO, KTO, ORPO, SimPO","the-dpo-family-ipo-kto-orpo-simpo",1,"IPO: fixes overfitting when preferences are almost certain · KTO: works with single thumbs-up or thumbs-down labels · ORPO: SFT and preference in one loss, no reference model · SimPO: length-normalized reward, no reference model"],[70,"GRPO and RL with verifiable rewards (RLVR)","grpo-and-rl-with-verifiable-rewards-rlvr",1,"Sample a group of answers for each prompt; normalize rewards inside the group · No critic model: a big memory saving · Verifiable rewards: math answers, unit tests, format checks · From DeepSeekMath to DeepSeek-R1; known issues and fixes (length bias, std normalization)"],[71,"Policy learning words: on-policy, off-policy, online, offline","policy-learning-words-on-policy-off-policy",1,"On-policy: learn from samples of the current policy (PPO, GRPO) · Off-policy and offline: learn from old or fixed data (DPO on a fixed set) · Reference policy, behavior policy, rollout, trajectory · Online and iterative DPO: mixing the two worlds"]]},{"name":"Reasoning, rewards & safety","s":[[72,"Reasoning models and test-time compute","reasoning-models-and-test-time-compute",1,"Chain-of-thought; why thinking tokens help · Self-consistency, best-of-N, majority vote · Scaling test-time compute vs scaling model size · Thinking mode in chat models; budget control; the latency cost"],[73,"Reward models, deeper: outcome rewards, process rewards, verifiers","reward-models-deeper-outcome-rewards-process-rewards",1,"Outcome reward models vs process reward models · LLM-as-judge as a reward; rubric rewards · Verifiers for code and math · Calibration and robustness of reward models"],[74,"Safety alignment: RLAIF and Constitutional AI","safety-alignment-rlaif-and-constitutional-ai",1,"AI feedback instead of human feedback · Constitutional AI: rules → critiques → revisions → preferences · Helpful vs harmless; over-refusal · Red-teaming basics"],[75,"RL infrastructure","rl-infrastructure",1,"Rollout generation is the bottleneck, so use a fast inference engine (vLLM, SGLang) · Weight sync between trainer and generator; shared vs separate GPUs · Synchronous vs asynchronous RL; off-policy drift · Libraries: TRL, verl, OpenRLHF, and what each is good for"],[76,"Lab: DPO on a small model","lab-dpo-on-a-small-model",1,"Build a preference dataset (chosen and rejected answers) · Train with TRL’s DPOTrainer; tune β · Measure win rate with an LLM judge; check for length bias · Compare with SFT only"],[77,"Lab: GRPO with a verifiable reward","lab-grpo-with-a-verifiable-reward",1,"A small model and math word problems with exact answers · Write reward functions for correctness and format · Train with GRPO; watch reward, answer length and KL · Read samples before and after: what did the model learn?"]]}]},{"id":"inference","name":"LLM inference and serving","cats":[{"name":"Generation, decoding & kernels","s":[[78,"The generation loop: prefill and decode","the-generation-loop-prefill-and-decode",1,"Prefill: read the whole prompt in parallel and build the KV cache (compute-bound) · Decode: one token per step, reusing the KV cache (memory-bound) · Why decode at batch 1 reads all weights to make about one token · Example from the survey: LLaMA-2-70B in FP16 needs 140 GB, and one token takes about 100 ms on 2 A100s"],[79,"Decoding strategies","decoding-strategies",1,"Greedy and beam search: where they work (translation) and fail (open text) · Temperature, top-k, top-p (nucleus), min-p · Repetition penalties and contrastive decoding · Constrained decoding with grammars and JSON schemas"],[80,"The KV cache","the-kv-cache",1,"Why we cache K and V, and not Q · The size formula, with worked examples · KV cache with MHA vs GQA vs MLA · Peak memory ≈ weights + KV cache: the main limit for long context and big batches"],[81,"Inference metrics: latency, memory and throughput","inference-metrics-latency-memory-and-throughput",1,"Latency: first-token latency (TTFT), per-output-token latency (TPOT), generation latency · Memory: model size, KV-cache size, peak memory · Throughput: tokens per second and requests per second; per user vs for the whole system · Goodput under a latency target (SLO); Little’s law"],[82,"Why inference is slow: three root causes and the roofline","why-inference-is-slow-three-root-causes",1,"Three costs: compute, memory access, memory size · Three root causes: model size, quadratic attention, token-by-token decoding · Roofline: prefill sits near the compute roof, decode sits on the bandwidth slope · Worked example: one 8B model on an NVIDIA L4 vs an H100"],[83,"The map: data-level, model-level and system-level optimization","the-map-data-level-model-level-and",1,"Data level: change the input or the shape of the output; the model stays the same · Model level: change the architecture or compress the weights; often lossy, may need training · System level: change the engine or the server; usually lossless · How the next 11 weeks follow this map, one branch at a time"],[84,"Batching basics and the latency–throughput trade-off","batching-basics-and-the-latency-throughput-trade",1,"Why batching helps decode: weights are read once for B tokens · Static and dynamic batching; padding waste · Plot: tokens/s and TPOT vs batch size; where the curve bends · Batch limits from KV-cache memory"],[85,"GPU kernels 1: CUDA basics for ML engineers","gpu-kernels-1-cuda-basics-for-ml",1,"Threads, warps, blocks, grids; occupancy · Global, shared and register memory; coalesced access; bank conflicts · Why fusion helps: fewer trips to HBM · Placing a kernel on the roofline"],[86,"GPU kernels 2: write kernels in the Triton language","gpu-kernels-2-write-kernels-in-the",1,"The programming model: blocks of data, not single threads · Write a fused softmax and an RMSNorm kernel; autotune block sizes · Compare with PyTorch eager and torch.compile · Name trap: the Triton language is not Triton Inference Server"],[87,"Lab: KV-cached generation, metrics and your GPU’s roofline","lab-kv-cached-generation-metrics-and-your",1,"Write generate() with and without a KV cache; measure the speedup · Measure TTFT and TPOT vs prompt length and batch size · Measure the memory bandwidth of your GPU and draw its roofline · Place prefill and decode on the roofline"]]},{"name":"Prompt & data-level compression","s":[[88,"Prompt pruning: Selective Context, LLMLingua, LongLLMLingua","prompt-pruning-selective-context-llmlingua-longllmlingua",1,"Remove tokens, sentences or examples that carry little information · Self-information from a small LM as the importance score · LLMLingua: budget controller, coarse-to-fine pruning, iterative token pruning · LongLLMLingua: question-aware scores and reordering for RAG prompts"],[89,"Prompt summary and soft-prompt compression: RECOMP, Gisting, AutoCompressors, ICAE","prompt-summary-and-soft-prompt-compression-recomp",1,"Prompt summary: a small model rewrites retrieved text into a short summary (RECOMP) · Soft prompts: learn a few vectors that act like a long prompt · Gisting: compress task instructions into gist tokens · AutoCompressors and ICAE: compress any context into memory slots"],[90,"RAG as input compression: FLARE, REPLUG, Self-RAG","rag-as-input-compression-flare-replug-self",1,"Instead of a huge prompt, retrieve only the relevant pieces · FLARE: retrieve when the model is about to be unsure · REPLUG: improve the retriever using the frozen LLM as the teacher · Cost math: long context vs retrieval (prefill tokens and KV cache)"],[91,"Output organization: Skeleton-of-Thought, APAR, SGLang; model cascades","output-organization-skeleton-of-thought-apar-sglang",1,"Skeleton-of-Thought: write a short outline, then expand all points in parallel as a batch · Graph-based and fork-based parallel decoding (SGD, APAR) · SGLang programs: share the KV cache of common prefixes automatically · Model cascades (FrugalGPT): try a small model first, call the big one only when needed"],[92,"Lab: Compress a long RAG prompt","lab-compress-a-long-rag-prompt",1,"Build a long-context QA test (many retrieved chunks) · Apply LLMLingua-style pruning at 2×, 5× and 10× compression · Measure tokens, TTFT and answer accuracy at each ratio · Compare with better retrieval (fewer, better chunks)"]]},{"name":"Efficient architectures","s":[[93,"Where the cost is: FFN and attention, by the numbers","where-the-cost-is-ffn-and-attention",1,"The survey reports the FFN holds 63% of LLaMA-7B parameters and 72% of LLaMA-70B parameters · FFN cost grows with model size; attention cost grows with context length · Compute your own model’s split: params, FLOPs and bytes per token · Which part to optimize for your workload (short chat vs long documents vs video)"],[94,"Efficient FFN 1: SwiGLU and parameter sharing (ALBERT)","efficient-ffn-1-swiglu-and-parameter-sharing",1,"SwiGLU: better quality per parameter; why d_{ff} becomes about 8d/3 · ALBERT: share one layer’s weights across all layers · ALBERT: factorized embeddings (|V|×E plus E×d instead of |V|×d) · What sharing saves (memory) and what it does not save (compute)"],[95,"Efficient FFN 2: MoE routing in depth","efficient-ffn-2-moe-routing-in-depth",1,"Top-k token-choice routing and the capacity factor · Load imbalance: why it wastes compute and padding · Fixes: load-balancing loss (Switch), router z-loss, BASE layers, expert-choice routing · Shared experts and fine-grained experts (DeepSeekMoE style)"],[96,"Efficient FFN 3: MoE from dense models, and MoE inference","efficient-ffn-3-moe-from-dense-models",1,"MoEfication: split a dense FFN into experts · Sparse upcycling: copy a dense FFN into many experts, then train · Inference: total vs active parameters; memory holds all experts · Batching and expert parallelism at serving time; Mixtral and DeepSeek as examples"],[97,"Efficient attention 1: MQA, GQA and MLA, from the KV-cache view","efficient-attention-1-mqa-gqa-and-mla",1,"Share K and V across heads (MQA) or groups of heads (GQA) · Uptraining: turn an MHA checkpoint into GQA by mean-pooling heads · MLA: store one small latent vector instead of full K and V · KV bytes per token and quality, side by side"],[98,"Efficient attention 2: low-rank attention (Linformer, Luna, Set Transformer)","efficient-attention-2-low-rank-attention-linformer",1,"The attention matrix is often close to low rank · Linformer: project K and V from length n to a fixed k · Luna and Set Transformer: a small set of learned queries summarizes the sequence (pack, then unpack) · Limits: causal masking and variable lengths are hard"],[99,"Efficient attention 3: kernel-based attention (Linear Transformer, Performer, RFA)","efficient-attention-3-kernel-based-attention-linear",1,"Replace softmax(qᵀk) with φ(q)ᵀφ(k) · Use associativity: compute φ(K)ᵀV once (d×d), then multiply by φ(Q) · Performer (FAVOR+) and RFA: random features that approximate softmax · Why quality drops: no sharp, peaked attention"],[100,"Transformer alternatives 1: state space models from HiPPO to S4","transformer-alternatives-1-state-space-models-from",1,"The linear state space recurrence · HiPPO: compress history into polynomial coefficients · S4, DSS, S4D, S5: diagonal matrices and fast convolution · Two views of one model: recurrence (fast decode) and convolution (parallel training)"],[101,"Transformer alternatives 2: Mamba, RWKV, RetNet, Hyena and hybrids","transformer-alternatives-2-mamba-rwkv-retnet-hyena",1,"Mamba: input-dependent (selective) A, B, C with a parallel scan · RWKV and RetNet: attention-like training, RNN-like decoding · Hyena: long convolutions with data-dependent kernels · Weakness at exact recall; hybrids such as Jamba mix attention and SSM layers"],[102,"Lab: Linformer, Performer and GQA against full attention","lab-linformer-performer-and-gqa-against-full",1,"Implement the three in one small GPT · Measure speed and memory for sequence lengths 512 to 16k · Measure quality (validation loss) and a copy task that needs exact recall · Explain each result with the formulas"]]},{"name":"Quantization","s":[[103,"Quantization math: scale, zero point, granularity and error","quantization-math-scale-zero-point-granularity-and",1,"Uniform affine quantization; symmetric vs asymmetric · Granularity: per-tensor, per-channel, per-group (for example 128), per-token · Rounding error vs clipping error; choosing the range (min-max, percentile, MSE) · Why LLMs are hard: outliers and wide ranges"],[104,"Where quantization helps: weight-only vs weight–activation","where-quantization-helps-weight-only-vs-weight",1,"Decode is GEMV and memory-bound: weight-only (W4A16, W8A16) helps · Prefill is GEMM and compute-bound: you need W8A8 or FP8 to use faster tensor cores · Survey test (A100, AWQ W4A16): decode about 2.0–2.4× faster at batch 1 and 1.2–1.4× at batch 16; prefill about 0.8–1.1×, often slower · The gain shrinks as batch and context grow; bigger models gain more"],[105,"Number formats: INT8, INT4, FP8, FP4, NF4 and microscaling","number-formats-int8-int4-fp8-fp4-nf4",1,"FP8 E4M3 vs E5M2; per-tensor scaling · FP4 and block-scaled formats (MX): a shared scale per small block · NF4 for normally distributed weights (QLoRA) · Hardware: which formats each GPU generation runs on tensor cores (for example, FP8 on L4 and H100) [check per GPU]"],[106,"Weight PTQ 1: from OBQ to GPTQ","weight-ptq-1-from-obq-to-gptq",1,"Layer-wise objective: keep WX close to ŴX on calibration data · The Hessian H = 2XXᵀ tells which weights matter · Quantize one column, then fix the remaining columns to cancel the error · GPTQ’s speed tricks: fixed order, lazy batch updates, Cholesky"],[107,"Weight PTQ 2: AWQ, outliers and mixed precision","weight-ptq-2-awq-outliers-and-mixed",1,"AWQ: protect weights connected to large activations by scaling them first · Search the scale per channel to minimize output error · Keep outliers in high precision: SpQR, OWQ; non-uniform levels: SqueezeLLM · Look-up-table kernels for non-uniform formats (LUT-GEMM)"],[108,"Weight–activation quantization: LLM.int8(), SmoothQuant, ZeroQuant, OmniQuant","weight-activation-quantization-llm-int8-smoothquant-zeroquant",1,"Activation outliers live in a few channels · LLM.int8(): run outlier channels in FP16, the rest in INT8 · SmoothQuant: move difficulty from activations to weights with a per-channel scale · ZeroQuant (group and token scales), OmniQuant (learned clipping), Atom (mixed precision)"],[109,"Rotations against outliers: QuIP, QuIP#, QuaRot, SpinQuant","rotations-against-outliers-quip-quip-quarot-spinquant",1,"Rotate weights and activations; the output does not change · A rotation spreads one big outlier over many channels · QuaRot: random Hadamard rotations, enables 4-bit weights, activations and KV · SpinQuant: learn the rotation on a small dataset"],[110,"KV-cache quantization: KIVI and KVQuant","kv-cache-quantization-kivi-and-kvquant",1,"The KV cache can be bigger than the weights at long context or big batch · Keys have channel outliers: quantize keys per channel, values per token (KIVI) · Non-uniform types and pre-RoPE keys (KVQuant) · Measure: long-context accuracy, not only perplexity"],[111,"Quantization-aware training: fake quant, STE, LLM-QAT, QLoRA, LoftQ","quantization-aware-training-fake-quant-ste-llm",1,"Fake quantization in the forward pass; straight-through estimator in the backward pass · LLM-QAT: data-free QAT by distilling from the FP16 model on its own generated text · QLoRA, QA-LoRA and LoftQ: low-bit base weights plus trainable low-rank adapters · When PTQ is enough and when QAT is worth the cost"],[112,"Lab: Compare RTN, GPTQ, AWQ, SmoothQuant and FP8 on one model","lab-compare-rtn-gptq-awq-smoothquant-and",1,"Quantize a 7B–8B model with each method · Measure perplexity, two tasks, prefill latency and decode latency on an L4 · Repeat at batch 1 and batch 16; compare with the survey’s table · Write a one-page recommendation for your production model"]]},{"name":"Pruning, sparsity & distillation","s":[[113,"Pruning basics: magnitude, OBD and OBS; structured vs unstructured","pruning-basics-magnitude-obd-and-obs-structured",1,"Magnitude pruning and its limits · Second-order pruning: OBD and OBS use the Hessian to choose and fix weights · Unstructured sparsity: high sparsity, little real speedup on GPUs · Structured sparsity (channels, heads, layers): real speedup, bigger accuracy loss"],[114,"Unstructured and 2:4 pruning: SparseGPT, Wanda, OWL","unstructured-and-2-4-pruning-sparsegpt-wanda",1,"SparseGPT: OBS-style pruning that scales to large models · Wanda: score = |weight| × norm of its input activation; no weight update · OWL: different sparsity per layer, based on outliers · 2:4 semi-structured sparsity: NVIDIA tensor cores skip half the weights"],[115,"Structured pruning: LLM-Pruner, Sheared LLaMA, SliceGPT, Minitron","structured-pruning-llm-pruner-sheared-llama-slicegpt",1,"Find coupled structures (heads, channels) and remove them together · Sheared LLaMA: prune to a target shape, then continue pretraining · SliceGPT: rotate, then delete whole rows and columns · Prune + distill (Minitron style); pruning experts in MoE models"],[116,"Sparse attention 1 (static): Sparse Transformer, Longformer, BigBird, StreamingLLM","sparse-attention-1-static-sparse-transformer-longformer",1,"Local (window), global, random and dilated patterns · BigBird: local + global + random keeps the model’s power · StreamingLLM: a window plus the first few tokens (attention sinks) · Static patterns also shrink the KV cache during decode"],[117,"Sparse attention 2 (dynamic): Reformer, Routing Transformer, SpAtten, H2O","sparse-attention-2-dynamic-reformer-routing-transformer",1,"Bucket tokens by hashing (Reformer) or clustering (Routing Transformer) · Prune unimportant tokens and heads on the fly (SpAtten) · H2O: keep recent tokens plus heavy hitters; evict the rest from the KV cache · Risk: once evicted, information is gone"],[118,"Structure optimization: NAS and low-rank factorization","structure-optimization-nas-and-low-rank-factorization",1,"Neural architecture search for transformers, and why it is costly for LLMs · Low-rank factorization with SVD · Activation-aware SVD (ASVD, SVD-LLM) · Mixing factorization with quantization and pruning"],[119,"Distillation for LLMs 1: white-box (logits and features)","distillation-for-llms-1-white-box-logits",1,"Forward KL (mode-covering) vs reverse KL (mode-seeking) · MiniLLM: reverse KL for generation · GKD: train on the student’s own outputs (on-policy distillation) · Feature and layer distillation; the capacity gap"],[120,"Distillation for LLMs 2: black-box (outputs only)","distillation-for-llms-2-black-box-outputs",1,"Distilling from API models: you only see the text · Distilling in-context learning and instruction following · Chain-of-thought distillation: Distilling step-by-step · Reasoning distillation into small models (DeepSeek-R1 style); filtering bad rationales"],[121,"Dynamic inference: early exit and layer skipping","dynamic-inference-early-exit-and-layer-skipping",1,"Sample-level early exit for classification: DeeBERT, FastBERT, PABEE · Token-level early exit for generation: CALM, SkipDecode · The KV-cache problem: skipped layers have no K and V for later tokens · Self-speculation with early-exit layers (LayerSkip)"],[122,"Lab: Prune with Wanda (2:4), recover with distillation","lab-prune-with-wanda-2-4-recover",1,"Prune a small LLM to 50% (unstructured) and to 2:4 · Measure perplexity and real speed with sparse kernels · Recover quality with a short distillation run · Report the trade-off curve"]]},{"name":"Operators, compilers & speculative decoding","s":[[123,"Runtime profiling: where the time goes","runtime-profiling-where-the-time-goes",1,"The survey’s profiling: attention and linear operators often take more than 75% of the time · Many tiny kernels: launch overhead and CPU gaps · Read a PyTorch profiler and an Nsight Systems trace · Find the top 3 time sinks of a real model"],[124,"Attention operator 1: FlashAttention 1, 2 and 3","attention-operator-1-flashattention-1-2-and",1,"Standard attention writes the T×T matrix to HBM; that is the slow part · Tiling + online softmax: attention block by block, inside SRAM · Recompute in backward instead of storing; FA2 work split; FA3 on Hopper (asynchrony, FP8) · Numerics: small differences from standard attention"],[125,"Attention operator 2: decode attention, FlashDecoding and FlashDecoding++","attention-operator-2-decode-attention-flashdecoding-and",1,"Decode has one query row: not enough parallel work for the GPU · FlashDecoding: split the KV sequence, compute parts in parallel, merge with log-sum-exp · FlashDecoding++: unified max value for softmax, flat-GEMM tuning · Paged-KV attention kernels"],[126,"Linear operators: GEMM, GEMV and flat GEMM","linear-operators-gemm-gemv-and-flat-gemm",1,"Prefill = GEMM (big matrices); decode = GEMV or flat GEMM (very thin matrices) · Tiling, split-K and padding waste for thin shapes · Quantized GEMM: dequantize inside the kernel (W4A16) · MoE grouped GEMM"],[127,"Graph-level optimization: fusion, CUDA graphs, torch.compile","graph-level-optimization-fusion-cuda-graphs-torch",1,"Fuse small ops (bias, activation, norm, residual) into one kernel · CUDA graphs remove CPU launch overhead in decode · torch.compile: graph capture, fusion, graph breaks · How TensorRT and inference engines fuse whole transformer layers"],[128,"Speculative decoding 1: the math","speculative-decoding-1-the-math",1,"A cheap draft proposes γ tokens; the target model checks them all in one pass · The acceptance rule keeps the target model’s exact distribution · Expected tokens per step from the acceptance rate α · When speculation does not pay: big batches, weak drafts"],[129,"Speculative decoding 2: drafts and verification (Medusa, EAGLE, Lookahead, SpecInfer)","speculative-decoding-2-drafts-and-verification-medusa",1,"Separate draft model vs self-drafting heads (Medusa) vs feature-level drafting (EAGLE) · Lookahead and prompt-lookup decoding: no draft model · Tree verification: check many candidate branches at once (SpecInfer) · Multi-token prediction heads used as drafters (MTP)"],[130,"Offloading: FlexGen, llama.cpp, PowerInfer","offloading-flexgen-llama-cpp-powerinfer",1,"When the model does not fit on the GPU: move weights or KV to CPU or SSD · FlexGen: plan where each tensor lives to maximize throughput · llama.cpp: split layers between CPU and GPU · PowerInfer: keep “hot” neurons on the GPU, “cold” ones on the CPU"],[131,"TensorRT and TensorRT-LLM in depth","tensorrt-and-tensorrt-llm-in-depth",1,"TensorRT: ONNX or network → optimized engine; layer fusion, kernel tuning, FP16, INT8, FP8 · Optimization profiles for dynamic shapes; an engine is tied to a GPU and a version · TensorRT-LLM: paged KV, in-flight batching, quantization, speculative decoding, plugins · When TensorRT-LLM beats vLLM, and when it is not worth the effort"],[132,"Lab: Profile, fuse and speculate","lab-profile-fuse-and-speculate",1,"Profile one decode step with Nsight Systems · Fuse RMSNorm + residual in Triton and measure · Run speculative decoding with a small draft and with EAGLE-style heads · Measure acceptance rate and tokens/s at batch 1, 4 and 16"]]},{"name":"Serving systems","s":[[133,"The serving stack and the life of a request","the-serving-stack-and-the-life-of",1,"Engine (vLLM, TensorRT-LLM, SGLang) → server (OpenAI API, Triton) → router → autoscaler · Life of a request: tokenize, queue, schedule, prefill, decode, detokenize, stream · Streaming, cancellation, timeouts · Serving many models and many LoRA adapters"],[134,"Memory management: PagedAttention, prefix caching, RadixAttention","memory-management-pagedattention-prefix-caching-radixattention",1,"Fragmentation with naive KV allocation · Paging: fixed-size blocks and a block table, like virtual memory · Sharing blocks across requests; copy-on-write for parallel sampling · Prefix caching and RadixAttention for repeated system prompts and documents"],[135,"Batching: continuous batching, chunked prefill, split-fuse","batching-continuous-batching-chunked-prefill-split-fuse",1,"Iteration-level (continuous) batching: requests join and leave every step (ORCA) · Chunked prefill: mix prefill chunks with decode steps (Sarathi, Sarathi-Serve) · Split-fuse in DeepSpeed-FastGen · Effect on TTFT, TPOT and throughput"],[136,"Scheduling: FCFS, preemption, fairness and SLOs","scheduling-fcfs-preemption-fairness-and-slos",1,"Head-of-line blocking with first-come-first-served · FastServe: preemptive multi-level feedback queue · Fairness between users: Virtual Token Counter (VTC) · SLO-aware scheduling and output-length prediction"],[137,"Distributed serving and prefill–decode disaggregation","distributed-serving-and-prefill-decode-disaggregation",1,"Tensor and pipeline parallel serving · Disaggregation: prefill GPUs and decode GPUs (Splitwise, DistServe, TetriInfer) · Moving the KV cache between GPUs; cost of transfer · Serving on cheap spot instances (SpotServe)"],[138,"vLLM internals","vllm-internals",1,"Scheduler and block manager; preemption by recompute or swap · CUDA graphs for decode; tensor-parallel and pipeline-parallel serving · Prefix caching, chunked prefill and speculative decoding inside vLLM · Key settings: max_num_seqs, max_model_len, gpu_memory_utilization"],[139,"Triton Inference Server, SGLang, LMDeploy, TGI, llama.cpp","triton-inference-server-sglang-lmdeploy-tgi-llama",1,"Triton Inference Server: model repository, config.pbtxt, backends (TensorRT, TensorRT-LLM, ONNX, PyTorch, Python), ensembles · SGLang and LMDeploy: what each does best · The survey compares inference engines and serving systems; how to read such tables · How to benchmark engines fairly on your own traffic"],[140,"Hardware accelerators and edge deployment","hardware-accelerators-and-edge-deployment",1,"GPUs vs FPGAs (FlightLLM) vs NPUs and other accelerators · Edge: memory, power and bandwidth limits · llama.cpp, MLX and mobile runtimes · Which survey techniques matter most at the edge"],[141,"Scenarios and capacity planning: agents, long context, edge, cost","scenarios-and-capacity-planning-agents-long-context",1,"Agents and multi-model pipelines: many calls, shared prefixes · Long context: prefill cost, KV memory, sparse attention · From traffic to GPUs: prompt and output lengths, latency target · Worked example: one model on an L4 vs an H100; cost per million tokens"],[142,"Lab: Benchmark a serving system","lab-benchmark-a-serving-system",1,"Serve a model with vLLM (optional: TensorRT-LLM on an L4) · Plot TTFT, TPOT and throughput vs load and context length · Turn on prefix caching, chunked prefill, FP8 or INT4, and speculative decoding, one at a time · Explain each change with the survey’s taxonomy"]]}]},{"id":"applications","name":"LLM applications","cats":[{"name":"Classic NLP tasks & metrics","s":[[143,"Prompting and in-context learning","prompting-and-in-context-learning",1,"Zero-shot, few-shot, instructions, system prompts · Why in-context learning works: the main theories · Prompt sensitivity: order and format of examples · Writing prompts as clear specifications"],[144,"Text classification: sentiment and beyond","text-classification-sentiment-and-beyond",1,"Encoder + CLS head, or decoder + label words, or embeddings + logistic regression · Zero-shot by comparing label likelihoods (“positive” vs “negative”); calibration · Class imbalance, thresholds, metrics: accuracy, F1, ROC-AUC · Long documents: split, classify, combine"],[145,"Natural language inference and question answering","natural-language-inference-and-question-answering",1,"NLI: entailment, contradiction, neutral (SNLI, MultiNLI); NLI models as zero-shot classifiers · Extractive QA: predict start and end positions in a passage (SQuAD) · Open-domain QA: retrieve, then read · Metrics: exact match (EM) and token F1"],[146,"Named entities, information extraction and structured output","named-entities-information-extraction-and-structured-output",1,"Token classification with BIO tags · Extraction with JSON schemas and function calling · Grammar-constrained decoding guarantees valid structure · When a small fine-tuned model beats a big prompted one"],[147,"Summarization","summarization",1,"Extractive vs abstractive · Encoder–decoder (BART, T5) vs decoder-only summarizers · Long documents: map-reduce, refine, hierarchical summaries · Faithfulness and hallucination in summaries"],[148,"Translation, code generation and dialogue","translation-code-generation-and-dialogue",1,"Machine translation with encoder–decoders and with LLMs · Code generation: execution-based testing · Dialogue: multi-turn context, chat templates, persona and memory · What makes each task hard to evaluate"],[149,"Metrics for text generation: perplexity, BLEU, ROUGE, METEOR, BERTScore, pass@k","metrics-for-text-generation-perplexity-bleu-rouge",1,"BLEU: clipped n-gram precision + brevity penalty · ROUGE-N and ROUGE-L (longest common subsequence) for summaries · METEOR (synonyms, stems) and BERTScore (embedding similarity) · pass@k for code, with the unbiased estimator"]]},{"name":"Search, RAG & agents","s":[[150,"Text embeddings and semantic search","text-embeddings-and-semantic-search",1,"Bi-encoder vs cross-encoder (reranker) · Pooling, normalization, Matryoshka embeddings · Public benchmarks (MTEB) vs your own data · Metrics: recall@k, MRR, nDCG"],[151,"Vector search at scale","vector-search-at-scale",1,"Exact search vs approximate nearest neighbor (ANN) search · IVF (clusters), PQ (compression), HNSW (graphs) · Memory and speed math; the recall vs latency trade-off · FAISS on CPU and GPU; filters and updates"],[152,"RAG, part 1: the basic pipeline","rag-part-1-the-basic-pipeline",1,"Why RAG: fresh, private and citable knowledge · Chunking, embedding, indexing; hybrid search (BM25 + dense) · Reranking and context assembly; citations · RAG vs long context vs fine-tuning"],[153,"RAG, part 2: advanced methods and evaluation","rag-part-2-advanced-methods-and-evaluation",1,"Query rewriting, HyDE, multi-query, multi-hop · GraphRAG and Self-RAG · Evaluate retrieval (recall@k) and generation (faithfulness, relevance) separately · Failure modes: wrong chunk, right chunk ignored, conflicting sources"],[154,"Agents and tool use","agents-and-tool-use",1,"ReAct: think → act → observe loops · Function calling, tool schemas, MCP · Planning, memory and multi-agent orchestration · Failure modes: loops, tool errors, prompt injection through tools; evaluation"]]},{"name":"Evaluation, reliability & safety","s":[[155,"Evaluation of LLM systems","evaluation-of-llm-systems",1,"Static benchmarks (MMLU, GSM8K) vs arenas vs your own test set · LLM-as-judge and its biases: position, length, self-preference · Statistics: confidence intervals and enough samples · A regression suite for your product"],[156,"Hallucination, calibration and uncertainty","hallucination-calibration-and-uncertainty",1,"Why models hallucinate: the training objective, missing knowledge, decoding · Detection: self-consistency, semantic entropy, retrieval checks · Calibration and abstaining (“I don’t know”) · Product designs that reduce hallucination"],[157,"Interpretability: looking inside the model","interpretability-looking-inside-the-model",1,"Logit lens: decode the residual stream at every layer · Attention patterns and induction heads · Probing classifiers · Sparse autoencoders and features"],[158,"Security and safety in LLM applications","security-and-safety-in-llm-applications",1,"Prompt injection, direct and indirect (through documents and tools) · Jailbreaks and adversarial suffixes · Data leakage and memorization · Guardrails, least-privilege tools, a human in the loop"]]},{"name":"Labs","s":[[159,"Lab: One model, five classic tasks","lab-one-model-five-classic-tasks",1,"Sentiment, NER, NLI, extractive QA and summarization on public datasets · Compare an encoder (BERT-style) with a small decoder LLM (prompted and LoRA-tuned) · Report the right metric for each task (F1, EM, ROUGE) · Note cost and latency per task"],[160,"Lab: Build and evaluate a RAG system","lab-build-and-evaluate-a-rag-system",1,"Index a document set with hybrid search and a reranker · Write a test set of 50 or more questions with known answers and sources · Measure retrieval recall@k and answer faithfulness · Fix the biggest failure mode and measure again"],[161,"Lab: An agent with tools, and its evaluation","lab-an-agent-with-tools-and-its",1,"Build a ReAct agent with search, a calculator and code execution · Write 30 tasks with checkable answers · Measure success rate, steps, cost and failure types · Add one guardrail against prompt injection and test it"],[162,"Lab: The whole LLM system on one whiteboard","checkpoint-the-whole-llm-system-on-one",1,"Draw the full path: tokenizer → model → training → serving → application · Answer exam-style questions with numbers: memory, FLOPs, KV cache, latency · Find the bottleneck in 5 short case studies, and name the survey branch that fixes it · List what is still unclear; we fix it before Part E"]]}]},{"id":"vision","name":"Vision and VLMs","cats":[{"name":"Vision encoders & CLIP","s":[[163,"Images as data and a CNN refresher","images-as-data-and-a-cnn-refresher",1,"Pixels, channels, normalization, resizing and aspect ratio · Convolution as a sliding filter; receptive field; pooling · From filters to CNN backbones (ResNet); transfer learning · What CNNs are good at, and what they miss"],[164,"The Vision Transformer (ViT)","the-vision-transformer-vit",1,"Patchify: cut the image into P×P patches; each patch becomes a token · Patch embedding: a linear layer, or a convolution with stride P · CLS token vs mean pooling; 2D position embeddings and interpolation · Tokens per image: resolution decides the cost"],[165,"Self-supervised vision: MAE, DINO, DINOv2, DINOv3","self-supervised-vision-mae-dino-dinov2-dinov3",1,"MAE: hide most patches, rebuild the pixels · DINO: a student matches a teacher on different views of the same image · DINOv2 and DINOv3: strong dense features for detection, segmentation, tracking · Global vs dense features: which to use for VLMs and for video"],[166,"CLIP: learning images and text together","clip-learning-images-and-text-together",1,"Two encoders, one shared space · Symmetric InfoNCE over a batch of image–text pairs; learned temperature · Zero-shot classification with prompts (“a photo of a {label}”) · Limits: counting, spatial relations, small text, web-data bias"],[167,"SigLIP and SigLIP 2","siglip-and-siglip-2",1,"Sigmoid loss: each image–text pair is its own yes/no question · No softmax over the whole batch: easier scaling and smaller batches · SigLIP 2: better localization and dense features, flexible-resolution variants, many languages · Why many open VLMs use a SigLIP-family encoder"],[168,"Open-vocabulary detection and segmentation","open-vocabulary-detection-and-segmentation",1,"Grounding: connect words to boxes and masks · OWL-ViT and Grounding DINO: detect anything you can name · SAM and SAM 2: promptable segmentation for images and video · How VLMs output boxes and points as text"],[169,"Vision applications: retrieval, zero-shot classification, near-duplicates","vision-applications-retrieval-zero-shot-classification-near",1,"Image retrieval with embeddings; indexing; re-ranking · Zero-shot vs few-shot vs linear probe for classification · Copy detection: global descriptors (SSCD) and robustness to edits · Metrics: recall@k, mAP, micro-AP"],[170,"Lab: Zero-shot classification and text-to-image search","lab-zero-shot-classification-and-text-to",1,"Run CLIP and SigLIP zero-shot on a labeled image set; test prompt templates · Build text-to-image search with a FAISS index · Measure recall@k; study the failures (counting, text, small objects)"],[171,"Lab: Compare vision features with linear probes","lab-compare-vision-features-with-linear-probes",1,"Extract DINOv2, CLIP and SigLIP features on the same dataset · Train linear probes; compare accuracy and speed · Test dense patch features on a simple localization task · Decide which encoder you would put in a VLM, and why"]]},{"name":"Early vision–language pretraining","s":[[172,"Two-stream and single-stream VLMs: ViLBERT, LXMERT, UNITER","two-stream-and-single-stream-vlms-vilbert",1,"Image regions from an object detector become visual tokens · Two streams with co-attention (ViLBERT, LXMERT) vs one joint stream (UNITER) · Pretraining tasks: masked words with the image, masked regions, image–text matching · Why they faded: slow detectors, small paired data, no generation"],[173,"ALBEF and BLIP: contrastive, matching and captioning losses together","albef-and-blip-contrastive-matching-and-captioning",1,"Image–text contrastive loss (ITC) aligns the two encoders · Image–text matching (ITM) with hard negatives checks fine details · Captioning loss (LM) lets the model write text · BLIP’s CapFilt: generate new captions and filter noisy web captions"],[174,"Grounded pretraining: GLIP and YOLO-World","grounded-pretraining-glip-and-yolo-world",1,"Detection rewritten as phrase grounding: match regions to words · Region–word alignment scores replace a fixed class head · YOLO-World: real-time open-vocabulary detection; encode the vocabulary once, then detect · Where these beat VLMs: speed, boxes, small objects"],[175,"Early video–language pretraining: VideoBERT, VideoCLIP, MERLOT","early-video-language-pretraining-videobert-videoclip-merlot",1,"VideoBERT: turn video features into visual words, then train BERT on video words + speech text · VideoCLIP: contrastive learning with overlapping clips and harder negatives · MERLOT: frames + transcripts; learn what happens and in what order · Lessons kept by modern Video-LLMs: speech as free labels, time alignment"],[176,"Lab: Compare CLIP, BLIP and BLIP-2 on retrieval, captioning and VQA","lab-compare-clip-blip-and-blip-2",1,"Run all three on the same small image set · Retrieval recall@k, caption quality (CIDEr or a judge), VQA accuracy · Look at where each one fails and why (architecture, losses, data)"]]},{"name":"VLM architecture","s":[[177,"Anatomy of a vision-language model","anatomy-of-a-vision-language-model",1,"Three parts: vision encoder → connector → language model · Two main families: one unified sequence, or cross-attention adapters · What is pretrained, what is new, and what is trained when · A tour of the design space"],[178,"Connectors: projector, Q-Former, Perceiver resampler, pixel shuffle","connectors-projector-q-former-perceiver-resampler-pixel",1,"Linear or MLP projector (LLaVA): simple and strong · Q-Former (BLIP-2): learned queries pull a fixed number of tokens from the image · Perceiver resampler (Flamingo): a fixed set of latents via cross-attention · Pixel shuffle and patch merging: fewer tokens by folding neighbors into channels"],[179,"Fusion: early, late, unified sequence and cross-attention","fusion-early-late-unified-sequence-and-cross",1,"Early vs late fusion, with examples · Unified sequence: image and text tokens in one self-attention stream · Cross-attention adapters with tanh gates: the LLM can stay frozen and safe · Trade-offs: tokens in the context vs extra layers"],[180,"High resolution and native aspect ratio","high-resolution-and-native-aspect-ratio",1,"Why 224×224 is not enough for documents, charts and small objects · Tiling (AnyRes): a thumbnail plus high-resolution crops · Dynamic resolution (Qwen2-VL): any size, 2×2 patches merged; NaViT-style packing · M-RoPE: separate position parts for time, height and width"],[181,"Case studies 1: LLaVA, BLIP-2, Flamingo, IDEFICS","case-studies-1-llava-blip-2-flamingo",1,"LLaVA: CLIP + MLP + LLM, trained on GPT-generated instruction data · BLIP-2: frozen encoder and frozen LLM; train only the Q-Former · Flamingo: interleaved images and text; few-shot multimodal learning · IDEFICS 2: what matters when building a VLM (ablations)"],[182,"Case studies 2: Qwen2.5-VL, InternVL, PaliGemma and Gemma, SmolVLM","case-studies-2-qwen2-5-vl-internvl",1,"Qwen2.5-VL: native-resolution ViT, window attention, absolute time for video · InternVL: a large vision encoder and dynamic tiling · PaliGemma and Gemma vision: SigLIP + Gemma, prefix-LM attention · SmolVLM: design choices for small and edge models"],[183,"Visual token efficiency","visual-token-efficiency",1,"Visual tokens often far outnumber text tokens, so they dominate cost · Token merging (ToMe) and pruning by attention (FastV) · Fixed-budget resamplers vs dynamic token counts · Measuring the accuracy vs cost trade-off honestly"],[184,"Audio and omni models","audio-and-omni-models",1,"Speech encoders (Whisper-style) and audio tokens · Aligning audio and video in time · Omni models: text, image, audio and video in; text and speech out · Applications: speaker detection, audio events, commentary understanding"],[185,"Lab: Build a mini-VLM","lab-build-a-mini-vlm",1,"Frozen SigLIP + MLP projector + small LLM · Stage 1: train only the projector on image–caption pairs · Stage 2: instruction-tune with LoRA on a small VQA set · Look at attention from text tokens to image tokens"]]},{"name":"Training & evaluating VLMs","s":[[186,"VLM training stages","vlm-training-stages",1,"Stage 1: alignment (projector only) · Stage 2: multimodal pretraining (unfreeze more) · Stage 3: instruction tuning; optional stage 4: preference tuning or RL · What to freeze, when, and why"],[187,"Multimodal data at scale","multimodal-data-at-scale",1,"Image–text pairs (LAION, DataComp), interleaved web documents (OBELICS), video–text · Filtering with CLIP score; deduplication; safety filtering · Synthetic and re-captioned data (dense captions) · The data mixture as a hidden hyperparameter; designing ablations"],[188,"Batching and masking for multimodal sequences","batching-and-masking-for-multimodal-sequences",1,"A different number of image tokens per sample · Packing samples, with attention masks so samples do not see each other · Loss only on the text answer, not on image tokens · Throughput: sort by length, balance tokens per GPU"],[189,"VLM SFT and PEFT","vlm-sft-and-peft",1,"Freeze or unfreeze the vision encoder; different learning rates per part · LoRA on the LLM, the connector, or the vision tower · QLoRA for VLMs · Keeping text-only skills while learning vision tasks"],[190,"VLM preference optimization and RL","vlm-preference-optimization-and-rl",1,"DPO and MPO for VLMs; preference data from humans or AI · Fine-grained corrections against hallucination (RLHF-V style) · GRPO for visual reasoning with checkable answers (counting, charts, geometry) · Reward design for vision tasks: IoU, exact match, format"],[191,"VLM tasks and how models answer them","vlm-tasks-and-how-models-answer-them",1,"Captioning, VQA, visual reasoning · Grounding and localization: boxes and points as text · Counting, OCR, charts, documents · Prompt formats for each task"],[192,"Evaluating VLMs","evaluating-vlms",1,"VQA accuracy, CIDEr for captions, ANLS for documents · MMMU, DocVQA, POPE (hallucination), MVBench and Video-MME · A domain test set sampled from your own data, with clear labels · Judge-based scoring for open answers, and its limits"],[193,"VLM failure modes","vlm-failure-modes",1,"Object hallucination: the language prior beats the image · Counting and spatial relations · Small text and fine details lost by resizing · Temporal confusion in video; causes and fixes for each"],[194,"Lab: LoRA fine-tune a small VLM","lab-lora-fine-tune-a-small-vlm",1,"Pick a small open VLM and a domain VQA dataset · Fine-tune with LoRA; compare frozen vs unfrozen vision tower · Evaluate before and after on held-out data; check text-only regressions"],[195,"Lab: Preference tuning a VLM against hallucination","lab-preference-tuning-a-vlm-against-hallucination",1,"Create chosen and rejected answers (correct vs hallucinated objects) · Train with DPO; tune β · Measure with a POPE-style yes/no test"]]},{"name":"VLM inference & serving","s":[[196,"The VLM inference path","the-vlm-inference-path",1,"Preprocessing on CPU or GPU: image decode and resize · Vision encoder cost and the long prefill of image tokens · Image tokens in the KV cache; encoder caching and prefix caching for repeated images · Where the time goes: a breakdown"],[197,"Quantizing VLMs","quantizing-vlms",1,"Why decode is bandwidth-bound and weight-only quantization helps · The VLM asymmetry: the vision encoder and connector are often more sensitive than the LLM · Outliers; calibration data must include images · Test quality after quantization on vision tasks, not only text"],[198,"Serving VLMs: vLLM, TensorRT-LLM and the edge","serving-vlms-vllm-tensorrt-llm-and-the",1,"Multimodal serving in vLLM and TensorRT-LLM; limits per request (images, frames) · Separating the encoder from prefill and decode · Edge: ONNX, transformers.js, llama.cpp with a vision projector, MLX · PEFT adapters on edge devices"],[199,"A latency budget on an NVIDIA L4: worked example","a-latency-budget-on-an-nvidia-l4",1,"Inputs: image size, frames, visual tokens, prompt and output length · Estimate encoder time, prefill time (compute-bound) and decode time (bandwidth-bound) · Choose resolution, frames, quantization and batch size to meet the budget · Check the estimate with a real measurement"],[200,"Lab: Serve a VLM and measure","lab-serve-a-vlm-and-measure",1,"Serve a small VLM with vLLM · Measure TTFT vs image resolution and number of video frames · Try FP8 or INT4 and prefix caching; record quality and speed"]]}]},{"id":"video","name":"Video and multimodal systems","cats":[{"name":"Video fundamentals","s":[[201,"Video as data","video-as-data",1,"Frames, fps, resolution; codecs and GOPs (I, P, B frames) · Decoding cost is often the hidden bottleneck; GPU decode (NVDEC) · Sampling: uniform, keyframes, scene-based, adaptive · Tokens per second of video: the budget problem"],[202,"Video encoders: from 3D CNNs to video transformers","video-encoders-from-3d-cnns-to-video",1,"I3D (inflated 2D filters) and SlowFast (two speeds); axial and factorized attention · ViViT and TimeSformer: divided space–time attention · VideoMAE: masked video pretraining with very high masking ratios · Video–text encoders such as InternVideo2"],[203,"Action recognition","action-recognition",1,"Clip-level vs video-level classification; datasets such as Kinetics · Temporal action localization: start, end and class · Zero-shot action recognition with video–text models · Sports actions are short, fast and fine-grained: what makes them hard"],[204,"Video object segmentation and tracking: from VOS to SAM 2","video-object-segmentation-and-tracking-from-vos",1,"Semi-supervised video object segmentation: mask in frame 1, follow the object · Memory-based matching across frames (XMem) · SAM 2: promptable segmentation with a streaming memory; points, boxes and masks as prompts · Referring segmentation from a sentence; metrics J and F"]]},{"name":"Video-LLMs & video tasks","s":[[205,"Video-LLMs","video-llms",1,"Frame sampling + per-frame encoder + temporal merging + LLM · Time encoding: frame index, timestamps as text, absolute time in M-RoPE · Video instruction data; joint image and video training · Case studies: Video-LLaVA, LLaVA-Video, Qwen2.5-VL video"],[206,"Long video","long-video",1,"Token compression across frames: merge similar tokens over time · Memory banks and streaming video LLMs · Agentic search: the model chooses which segments to look at · Captions as a compressed memory of the video"],[207,"Temporal grounding and key-moment detection","temporal-grounding-and-key-moment-detection",1,"Moment retrieval: given text, return the start and end time · Highlight detection: score each clip by importance · DETR-style detectors (Moment-DETR) vs LLMs that write timestamps (TimeChat) · Metrics: R1@IoU and mAP; label noise in “key moments”"],[208,"Text-to-video retrieval","text-to-video-retrieval",1,"Frame embeddings + pooling (CLIP4Clip): mean, sequential, attention · Clip-level vs segment-level indexes; choosing the segment length · Video–text encoders (ViCLIP, InternVideo2) · Scaling to millions of segments; re-ranking with a VLM"],[209,"Video fingerprinting and near-duplicate detection","video-fingerprinting-and-near-duplicate-detection",1,"Goal: the same content after crop, resize, re-encode, logo or speed change; not the same meaning · Perceptual hashes vs learned per-frame descriptors (SSCD-style) · Temporal alignment of matches (similarity matrices, ViSiL-style) · Evaluation: precision–recall, micro-AP, robustness tests"],[210,"Video question answering and Video-RAG","video-question-answering-and-video-rag",1,"Retrieve then read: find the right segments, then let a VLM answer · Captions, ASR transcripts and OCR as searchable memory · Indexes at several levels: shot, scene, whole game · When to use Video-RAG vs a long-context video model"],[211,"Production video understanding pipelines","production-video-understanding-pipelines",1,"Shot and scene boundary detection · Captioning and classification per shot or scene, for example IAB and brand-safety labels · Active speaker detection with audio–visual fusion · Streaming vs batch pipelines; latency, cost and monitoring"],[212,"Training video-language models","training-video-language-models",1,"Video–text data: ASR-aligned, captioned, synthetic · Temporal annotations and dense captions · Domain fine-tuning (for example sports) with LoRA · Efficiency: fewer frames, lower resolution, token merging, packing"],[213,"Evaluating video understanding","evaluating-video-understanding",1,"Multiple-choice video QA (Video-MME, MVBench) · Grounding (R1@IoU) and retrieval (R@k) metrics · Long-video pitfalls: answers guessed from text alone · Building a domain video test set"]]},{"name":"Video labs","s":[[214,"Lab: Text-to-video retrieval","lab-text-to-video-retrieval",1,"Cut videos into segments; embed frames with SigLIP; pool per segment · Index with FAISS and search with text queries · Measure recall@k; compare mean pooling with a small temporal model"],[215,"Lab: Key-moment detection","lab-key-moment-detection",1,"Score each segment with (a) similarity to event prompts and (b) a VLM yes/no question · Combine the scores; pick top moments with non-maximum suppression in time · Evaluate against labeled moments with tIoU-based metrics"],[216,"Lab: Video fingerprinting","lab-video-fingerprinting",1,"Build per-frame descriptors and a temporal matcher · Attack your own system: crop, resize, re-encode, speed up, add a logo · Plot precision–recall and find the weakest transform"],[217,"Lab: Segment and track players with SAM 2","lab-segment-and-track-players-with-sam",1,"Prompt SAM 2 with boxes from a detector on a sports clip · Track through occlusions and camera cuts; find where it fails · Measure J&F on a few hand-labeled frames"]]},{"name":"Documents, multimodal RAG & agents","s":[[218,"Document AI","document-ai",1,"OCR pipelines vs OCR-free models (Donut, VLMs) · Layout, tables, charts; parsing documents to markdown · DocVQA and information extraction · Page-image retrieval with multi-vector embeddings (ColPali)"],[219,"Multimodal RAG","multimodal-rag",1,"Indexes for text, images, video segments and document pages · Cross-modal retrieval; late interaction vs single vectors · Feeding retrieved images and frames to a VLM; token budgets · Evaluation across modalities"],[220,"Agentic VLMs and vision-language-action models","agentic-vlms-and-vision-language-action-models",1,"Computer-use agents: read screenshots, click, type · Tool-using VLMs: crop, zoom, detect, search · VLA models (RT-2, OpenVLA, π0): actions as tokens or continuous outputs · Evaluation and safety for agents that act"],[221,"Lab: Multimodal RAG over PDFs","lab-multimodal-rag-over-pdfs",1,"Index PDF pages as images (ColPali-style) and as text · Answer questions with a VLM using the retrieved pages · Compare text-only RAG and page-image RAG on tables and charts"]]}]},{"id":"generative","name":"Generative models","cats":[{"name":"Generative model families","s":[[222,"The map of generative models: autoregressive, VAE, GAN, diffusion, flow","the-map-of-generative-models-autoregressive-vae",1,"What each family learns and how it samples · Likelihood, sample quality and speed: the trade-off triangle · Where LLMs (autoregressive) and image generators (diffusion) meet · Metrics: FID, CLIP score, human preference"],[223,"Variational autoencoders and latent spaces","variational-autoencoders-and-latent-spaces",1,"Encoder, decoder and the reparameterization trick · The ELBO: reconstruction plus a KL term · Why latent diffusion works in a VAE latent space · Posterior collapse and blurry samples"],[224,"Visual tokenizers for generation","visual-tokenizers-for-generation",1,"VQ-VAE: map image patches to entries of a codebook (discrete tokens) · VQGAN: sharper codes with adversarial and perceptual losses · Discrete vs continuous visual tokens: understanding vs generation · Why this matters for any-to-any models"]]},{"name":"Diffusion & flow","s":[[225,"Diffusion 1: the forward noising process and the reverse denoiser (DDPM)","diffusion-1-the-forward-noising-process-and",1,"Add Gaussian noise step by step; the closed form for any step t · Train a network to predict the noise · Sampling: start from pure noise and denoise step by step · Noise schedules and why they matter"],[226,"Diffusion 2: score matching, DDIM and fast samplers","diffusion-2-score-matching-ddim-and-fast",1,"The score: the direction that makes data more likely · Diffusion as a differential equation (score SDE and probability-flow ODE) · DDIM: deterministic sampling with far fewer steps · Modern solvers and the step-count trade-off"],[227,"Guidance and conditioning: classifier guidance, classifier-free guidance, ControlNet","guidance-and-conditioning-classifier-guidance-classifier-free",1,"Conditioning on text through cross-attention · Classifier guidance and classifier-free guidance; the guidance scale · Why high guidance gives sharp but less varied images · ControlNet: add spatial control (edges, pose, depth)"],[228,"Latent diffusion and diffusion transformers (DiT)","latent-diffusion-and-diffusion-transformers-dit",1,"Diffuse in a compressed latent space, not in pixels · The U-Net vs the transformer backbone (DiT) · Patchify latents; adaLN conditioning · Scaling behavior of DiT models"],[229,"Flow matching and rectified flow","flow-matching-and-rectified-flow",1,"Learn a velocity field that moves noise to data · Straight paths are easier to integrate: rectified flow · The simple regression loss of flow matching · Rectified-flow transformers in modern image models"],[230,"Few-step generation: consistency models and distillation","few-step-generation-consistency-models-and-distillation",1,"Why 50 steps is too slow for products · Progressive distillation: halve the steps again and again · Consistency models: jump from any noise level to the clean image · Quality vs steps vs latency"]]},{"name":"Video & any-to-any generation","s":[[231,"Video generation: temporal coherence, video DiTs and evaluation","video-generation-temporal-coherence-video-dits-and",1,"From images to video: space-time latents and attention · Keeping objects consistent across frames · Evaluation: FVD, human preference, physics and motion checks · Cost: tokens, memory and latency for video"],[232,"Native multimodal and any-to-any models","native-multimodal-and-any-to-any-models",1,"One shared vocabulary of discrete image tokens (Chameleon, Emu3) · Hybrid: next-token text + diffusion for images in one model (Transfusion) · Separate encoders for understanding and for generation (Janus) · Monolithic vs factorized output heads; balancing tasks in the data"],[233,"Lab: Train a tiny diffusion model and a tiny flow-matching model","lab-train-a-tiny-diffusion-model-and",0,"Train both on 2D toy data, then on small images · Plot sample paths: curved (diffusion) vs straight (rectified flow) · Compare quality vs number of sampling steps"]]}]},{"id":"capstone","name":"Capstone and interviews","cats":[{"name":"Capstone & interviews","s":[[234,"Dissect a modern open model","dissect-a-modern-open-model",1,"Pick one, for example Gemma 4, Qwen3, DeepSeek-V3 or Qwen2.5-VL · From the config: layers, heads, KV heads, experts, vision tower, context length · Compute parameters, FLOPs per token, KV cache per token, memory to serve · Read its technical report and explain every design choice with what you learned"],[235,"Capstone design: sports key-moment search","capstone-design-sports-key-moment-search",1,"Requirements: queries, latency, accuracy, cost, hardware (L4 + TensorRT) · Design: segmenting, embedding, indexing, VLM re-ranking, timestamps · Latency and cost math before building · Risks, failure modes, and what to measure"],[236,"Lab: Capstone build","lab-capstone-build",0,"Build the index and the retrieval service · Add VLM re-ranking and timestamp refinement · Serve with vLLM or TensorRT-LLM; profile and fix the bottleneck"],[237,"Lab: Capstone evaluation and failure analysis","lab-capstone-evaluation-and-failure-analysis",0,"Evaluate retrieval and grounding on your own labeled set · Error analysis: group the failures by cause · Write a short technical report with numbers, limits and next steps"],[238,"Interview drills: ML system design for LLM, VLM and video services","interview-drills-ml-system-design-for-llm",1,"A repeatable answer frame: requirements, numbers, design, bottleneck, failure modes · Drill 1: a chat API with a latency target · Drill 2: natural-language video search over a sports archive · Drill 3: real-time key-moment alerts on live broadcast"],[239,"Final review and what to read next","final-review-and-what-to-read-next",1,"Review the 7 parts with one hard question each · Open problems: long video, efficient reasoning, unified models, evaluation · Your reading list for the next 6 months · How to keep up: surveys, conferences, code"]]}]}];
